he context that lives in a senior engineer's head isn't in any prompt. Code review becoming a teaching forum rather than a checkpoint feels like the right reframe for this moment.","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Huy Nguyen"},"interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":5}},{"@type":"Comment","datePublished":"2026-04-20T07:10:34.007Z","text":"\u003EA junior prompts an agent, a full pull request lands, and the question is: who reviews it? A senior? Another agent?\nwhat do you think about the solution of a senior pairing with a junior and scrapping the code review? Would that not solve the issue upstream, reduce tech debt in the long term, ensure the architecture is correct, and reduce total bugs(two minds are better than one), and also allow the junior to level up quickly because they're in the trenches with someone more experienced. This could even work for 2 seniors working together too. From my limited experience this seems to be the logical solution, but i am aware that perhaps the structure of some organisations may prevent this from happening, or maybe it's not possible for some other reasonreally curious as to your thoughts considering your extensive experience","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Ali Reda"},"interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":4}},{"@type":"Comment","datePublished":"2026-04-20T14:15:31.109Z","text":"I enjoyed your chat and post.\n\nMy attention was caught by this particular statement:\n\"Reading code is going to be the new skill ... but it's always been the skill\"\nIt's the \"it's always been the skill\" that got me :).\n\nI would be interested in your opinion about Moldable Development: a way of programming optimized for understanding through contextual deterministic tools - without relying on reading as the primary source of extracting information from systems. I am writing a book about it with Simon Wardley in the open at: moldabledevelopment.com\n\nInterested to have a conversation?","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Tudor Girba"},"interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":6}},{"@type":"Comment","datePublished":"2026-04-20T06:49:18.995Z","text":"Yes, solving it vs. solving it in the specific way you need are very different things. AI gets you to \"it works\" quickly, but code reviews, sophisticated guardrails (and Straion) is what ensures it actually fits your system’s history, constraints, and intent.","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Katrin Freihofner"},"interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":5}},{"@type":"Comment","datePublished":"2026-04-20T06:41:20.149Z","text":"Telemetry, extended logging and monitoring   Along with extensive code review. ","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Gokul Dhamodaran"},"interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":5}},{"@type":"Comment","datePublished":"2026-04-20T12:27:27.884Z","text":"The PR size thing is the quiet killer. Reviewed one last week where the agent rewrote three utilities we already had because it didn't grep the repo first. Took me longer to review than it would've taken to write. \"Tell me why this is the best way\" is going in my workflow today. Addy Osmani","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Tornike Nizharadze"},"interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":2}}],"commentCount":100,"caption":{"@type":"MediaObject","contentUrl":"https://dms.licdn.com/playlist/vid/v2/D5605AQHhs6l685Lz7A/video-auto-caption-srt-acs-singleton/B56Z2pmCcdKIEg-/0/1776666853279?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=P9s3cuX5uLxVKF173bfv8EiODdnMaKmdwOy4Y3i1Vh8","description":"Video Captions for Code